    {
      "id": "GENUS_BRADYPUS",
      "name": "Bradypus",
      "rank": "GENUS",
      "description": "Three-toed sloths — the slowest mammals on Earth and the vertebrates with the lowest recorded metabolic rate for their body size. Sloths spend 15–20 hours per day hanging upside down and sleeping, descending to the forest floor just once a week to defecate — a slow, conspicuous, and dangerous process that accounts for a high proportion of all sloth deaths. Their organs are anchored to their ribs rather than the spine, so hanging inverted requires no muscular effort. A greenish alga grows in grooves in their fur, providing camouflage and perhaps supplementary nutrition; sloth fur also hosts a dedicated ecosystem of moths, beetles, and mites found nowhere else. Three-toed sloths cannot regulate their temperature well and depend on moving between sun and shade to thermoregulate.",
      "lineage": "Three-toed Sloths",
